From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Ericson <John.Ericson@Obsidian.Systems>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 12:48:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] virtiofs: `FUSE_RELEASE`/`FUSE_RELEASEDIR` must be given a
 reply buffer

`virtiofs_call()` inferred "this request has no reply" from the shape of
its arguments: `outbodylen == 0 && data == NULL`.  That is true of
`FUSE_FORGET`, which really is unanswered, but it is also true of
`FUSE_RELEASE` and `FUSE_RELEASEDIR`, which are answered with a bare
`struct fuse_out_header`.  For those two we therefore built a chain with
no device-writable descriptor at all.

virtiofsd handles `FUSE_RELEASEDIR` with `reply_ok(None::<u8>, None,
..)`, which does `w.write_all(header.as_slice())` into a `Writer` built
from the chain's writable descriptors -- of which there were none.  The
write consumes zero bytes, `write_all` fails with `WriteZero`, and the
whole thing comes back as

    failed to encode fuse message: failed to write whole buffer

That is not a per-request error: virtiofsd propagates it out of
`process_message()` and tears the vhost-user connection down, so qemu
then dies with "Unexpected end-of-file before all data were read".

`ls` is the first thing that trips it, because closedir(3C) is the first
`FUSE_RELEASEDIR` we ever send -- mounting only needs `FUSE_INIT`,
`FUSE_GETATTR`, `FUSE_STATFS` and friends, all of which have fixed size
replies and so were given a bounce buffer.  `FUSE_READDIR` itself is
fine: it supplies a 16 byte writable bounce descriptor followed by the
8 KiB data descriptor, and virtiofsd's `readdir()` splits exactly
`size_of::<OutHeader>()` off the front of the writer, so the header and
the dirent stream land where we expect.  The descriptor granularity
question is a red herring; virtiofsd's `Writer` treats the writable
section as a byte stream, as the author guessed.

Decide "no reply" from the opcode instead, which is the only thing that
actually determines it.

Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5 (claude-opus-5)
---
 usr/src/uts/common/fs/virtiofs/virtiofs_subr.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/usr/src/uts/common/fs/virtiofs/virtiofs_subr.c b/usr/src/uts/common/fs/virtiofs/virtiofs_subr.c
index e699013d37..2dfd81dbdd 100644
--- a/usr/src/uts/common/fs/virtiofs/virtiofs_subr.c
+++ b/usr/src/uts/common/fs/virtiofs/virtiofs_subr.c
@@ -118,7 +118,17 @@ virtiofs_call(vfsmnt_t *vfs, uint32_t opcode, uint64_t nodeid,
 	char *obuf, *ibuf;
 	size_t obuflen = sizeof (*ihdr) + inbodylen;
 	size_t ibuflen;
-	boolean_t noreply = (outbodylen == 0 && data == NULL);
+	/*
+	 * FUSE_FORGET is the only request we send that the server does not
+	 * answer at all.  Every other opcode is answered with at least a
+	 * fuse_out_header even when it has no out-args of its own --
+	 * FUSE_RELEASE and FUSE_RELEASEDIR are the ones we send -- so a
+	 * device-writable buffer has to be supplied for it.  Leaving one out
+	 * does not merely lose the reply: the server has nowhere at all to
+	 * write, which it treats as a fatal encoding failure and drops the
+	 * connection.
+	 */
+	boolean_t noreply = (opcode == FUSE_FORGET);
 	size_t got;
 	int r;
 
